<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Istanbul</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Istanbul is the city on the Bosporus formerly known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1453. It became the capital of the Ottoman Empire and one of the largest and most cosmopolitan cities of the world. It remained the imperial centre until the empire's end after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/istanbul.jpg" alt="Istanbul" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hunanuk · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Historical_peninsula_and_modern_skyline_of_Istanbul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Khans who defied <b>Istanbul</b> could be removed, and some ruled several separate times.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1574 an Ottoman fleet and army under Sinan Pasha and Uluj Ali took Tunis and La Goulette, the last Hafsid ruler was taken to <b>Istanbul</b>, and Ifriqiya became Ottoman Tunisia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The architect Mimar Sinan is credited with more than three hundred buildings, including the Süleymaniye Mosque in <b>Istanbul</b> and the Selimiye Mosque in Edirne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After the coastal ports fell to the Ottomans in 1484 the economy turned inwards, and from the sixteenth century the sultans demanded grain, livestock and timber for <b>Istanbul</b> at fixed prices, a burden that grew heavy in the Phanariot period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From about 1711 the dey also held the title of pasha, ending the sultan’s appointed governors, and Algiers conducted its own wars and treaties while recognising Ottoman suzerainty and sending gifts to <b>Istanbul</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Lausanne of 24 July 1923 superseded Sèvres and recognised the Turkish state as the empire's successor; the last Allied troops left <b>Istanbul</b> on 4 October, and on 29 October 1923 the Republic was proclaimed in Ankara, the new capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
